WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.493
The integration problem.

2
00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:07.640
In Studio 1 you gave a model tools by writing function definitions into your own application.

3
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.861
That works well for one application.

4
00:00:10.141 --> 00:00:26.739
Now picture a team that uses four AI applications (a chat assistant, a coding agent, an IDE plugin, an internal bot) and wants each to reach six systems (the issue tracker, the wiki, the CRM, the data warehouse, the calendar, the file share).

5
00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:37.557
If every application writes its own connector to every system, that is 4 times 6, or 24 integrations, each with its own auth handling, bugs, and maintenance.

6
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.269
Add a seventh system and you write four more.

7
00:00:40.549 --> 00:00:55.675
This is the N-by-M problem, and it is what Anthropic named when it introduced the Model Context Protocol in November 2024: every new data source needed its own custom implementation, so connected systems were hard to scale.

8
00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:58.685
A shared protocol turns the product into a sum.

9
00:00:58.965 --> 00:01:08.075
Each application implements the client side once, each system implements a server once, and any pair can talk: 4 plus 6, or 10 pieces of work.

10
00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:17.101
The specification says it took inspiration from the Language Server Protocol, which did the same thing for programming-language support across editors.

11
00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:23.376
The MCP docs use a hardware analogy, a USB-C port for AI applications.

12
00:01:23.656 --> 00:01:25.320
Hold the arithmetic loosely.

13
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.480
A protocol makes connections possible, not good.

14
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:36.227
Servers still vary in quality, hosts support different subsets of the protocol, and models differ in how well they use tools.

15
00:01:36.507 --> 00:01:43.461
MCP removes duplicated plumbing; it does not remove the need to test each combination you depend on.
